! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1262 ("TEST EX AND EXIC"), TEST 1C's
! header row and its first two blocks, TEST 1CA and TEST 1CB --
! PDF p.92, octal 023442.40-023460.00.  27 SOURCE ROWS over 28
! half-words (the TI at 023454.00 is a full word), entry label I6218,
! second entry label I6219.  Checked bit-exact against the listing's
! printed machine code by verify_ax5b_162_test1cab_listing.py.
!
! CITED BY PDF PAGE.  jb_ax5b's LISTING pages carry no printed page
! numbers -- the "12 11 10 9" down the left margin is fanfold
! carriage-tape line numbering, and the only printed numbers are in the
! front write-up, which is scanned 2-up.  Every verify_ax5b_*.py here
! already says "PDF p.NN" for that reason.
!
! WHERE THIS SITS.  It is a gap between two files that were already
! here: verify_ax5b_162_test1b_listing.py ends at 023442.00
! ("SX,$X13,IC262", 1B's continuity update) and
! verify_ax5b_162_test1cc_listing.py begins at 023474.40 (I6225).
! 023442.40 is the very next half-word after 1B's last row, so the two
! ranges abut with nothing between them.  Left behind on the far side:
! 1CC's first subject class, 023460.40-023474.00.  No other verify file
! in this repo covers any address in 023442.40-023460.00; the only
! overlap a plain octal scan reports is verify_pau_chkssr_listing.py,
! which is a DIFFERENT DECK (jb_af1c, the PAU gates routine) that
! happens to load at the same octal addresses -- its rows are square
! roots, not EX.
!
! WHAT THESE TWO BLOCKS ARE.  They are the executable form of Reference
! Manual printed p.50 (PDF p.53), read first-hand at 400 dpi, the
! paragraph that continues from p.49's "In general, when the
! interruption system is dis-":
!
!   "abled, interruption cannot occur and address monitoring is
!   suspended.  In order that control may remain entirely with the main
!   program during an execution operation, two special exceptions are
!   made.  First, address monitoring is always in force during (1)
!   EXECUTE and (2) EXECUTE INDIRECT AND COUNT, except for the address
!   of the location of the FIRST LEVEL pseudo-instruction counter.
!   Second, at the completion, termination, or suppression of an
!   execution operation, interruption is always permitted."
!
! and its Programming Note directly below, which bounds exception 2:
!
!   "When interruption occurs following an execution operation with the
!   interruption system disabled, it is caused only by the actuated
!   indicator of highest priority whose mask bit is one.  Further
!   interruptions cannot occur until either the system is enabled or
!   another execution operation is completed, terminated, or
!   suppressed.  Examination for other actuated indicators can be
!   programmed into the correction routine.
!
!   Any actuated indicator whose mask bit is on may cause interruption
!   at the completion, termination, or suppression of an execution
!   operation."
!
! 1CA tests the SECOND exception alone; 1CB tests the FIRST with the
! second as its delivery mechanism.  The deck names them itself:
! "-TEST 1CA, ENABLE AFTER EX." and "-TEST 1CB, CHK ADR MONITOR."
!
! THE BLOCK IS DISABLED, on three independent grounds, all from rows in
! this file:
!   (i)   023442.40 is "BD,$+.32" -- BRANCH DISABLED -- and it is the
!         first instruction of TEST 1C.
!   (ii)  no BE appears anywhere in 023442.40-023460.00.  (The next BE
!         on the page is at 023514.00, deep inside 1CC.)
!   (iii) both SVAs name "I62ITE" and NOT "I62ITE+.32".  PDF p.109
!         prints I62ITE as a full word holding "BD,$" at 024320.00 and
!         "B,$" at 024320.40 (verify_ax5b_162_int_entry_listing.py),
!         so a landing in the .00 half DISABLES on arrival.  1CB is
!         therefore entered still disabled and needs no BD of its own,
!         and it has none.
! Corroborating but not needed: "BD,I60S1" at 023443.40 calls 1260's
! clear-indicators subroutine, whose return is "$BD,0" at 023041.00
! (ax5b_160s1_clr_inds.strap's header, from PDF p.85), so the call
! cannot leave the system enabled either.
!
! HOW 1CA WORKS, AND WHAT THE "-ILLEGAL OP CODE." WORD ACTUALLY DOES.
! The word at 023445.40 is not an operand and is not the EX's subject.
! It sits IN THE STRAIGHT-LINE INSTRUCTION STREAM, between "SV,$X1,$IA"
! and "LVI,$X1,I6219", and is executed on the way past.  Reference
! Manual printed p.45 (PDF p.48), item 15, read first-hand:
! "Operation Code Invalid (OP).  The instruction just attempted used an
! operation code or combination of modifiers which is not defined.  An
! instruction with invalid operation code is executed as NO OPERATION."
! Indicator OP is 15, and printed p.48 (PDF p.51) says of the mask
! word: "Not all of the 64 mask bits can be set by program control.
! Those corresponding to indicators 0-19 are permanently set to 1."
! So OP is actuated and masked-on -- but the system is DISABLED, so
! nothing interrupts there.
!
! The word's ROLE HERE IS A PRE-CHARGE: it arms OP in advance so that
! the EX's completion boundary has something to deliver.  That is a
! THIRD role for the same 00000003740 word in this deck.  In 1CC
! (023506.00 and 023520.40) it is an ENABLE-STATE PROBE, used in
! opposite directions on the two sides of the same test; in TEST 3A/3B
! (024146.00, 024162.40) it is a HIGHER-PRIORITY DECOY that must not
! win against AD.  Here it is neither: nothing measures the enable
! state and nothing competes with OP.
!
! Three things make the pre-charge reading the deck's own, not an
! inference from usage:
!   * the error text says so.  "-NO INT TAKEN ON EX / -WHEN OP SET."
!     asserts that OP was already SET when the EX ran.
!   * the vector is planted AFTER the illegal word, not before.
!     "LVI,$X1,I6219 / SVA,$X1,I62ITE" are at 023446.00 and 023446.40,
!     downstream of 023445.40.  If the illegal word could interrupt
!     where it stands, control would go to whatever TEST 1B last left
!     in the landing word (I6217, at 023433.00) and the block would
!     break rather than report.
!   * nothing between 023445.40 and 023447.00 clears OP.  "Z,$IND" is
!     at 023444.00, UPSTREAM of the illegal word; the two rows after it
!     are an LVI and an SVA.
!
! THE SUBJECT OF "EX,$-.32" IS THE SVA, AND THE PRINT SETTLES IT.
! 023447.00 prints "23446.52 00"; the octal pair 52 is 101010, so bit
! 18 -- the top bit of the pair, and the half-word select of a 19-bit
! effective address -- is ONE, giving 023446.40.  That is
! "SVA,$X1,I62ITE", not the illegal word (which is three half-words
! back, i.e. "$-1.32").  The subject is IDEMPOTENT, provably so: $X1
! still holds I6219 because nothing between 023446.00 and 023447.00
! touches it, so re-executing the SVA stores the same address into the
! same landing word.  The subject is chosen to do nothing so that the
! only thing under test is the boundary: at the COMPLETION of the
! execution operation, the already-actuated OP must cause an
! interruption although the system is disabled.  The interruption lands
! at I62ITE (024320.00, "BD,$" with its address rewritten), which
! branches to I6219 -- the start of 1CB.  So 1CA's PASS PATH IS THE
! ENTRY TO 1CB, and falling through instead reports
! "-NO INT TAKEN ON EX WHEN OP SET."
!
! HOW 1CB WORKS.  "LX,$X1,BIT57 / SX,$X1,3.0" puts the boundary-control
! bit into word 3; "LVI,$X1,I62EX / SV,$X1,$UB" sets the upper boundary
! to 024322.00.  The subject, I62EXI, is at 024332.00 -- ABOVE the
! upper boundary, i.e. in the protected area.  "Z,$X1" clears the index
! register the subject would write.  "TI,1,I00O,$MASK" transmits the
! all-ones index word into the mask, i.e. turns ON EVERY SETTABLE MASK
! BIT (20-47); it is needed here and not in 1CA because IF is indicator
! 21, outside the permanently-masked 0-19 range that OP sits in.  Then
! "EX,I62EXI" -- an EX whose subject lies in the protected area, with
! the system disabled.
!
! Required behaviour, and the deck checks both halves: p.50 exception 1
! keeps address monitoring in force, so IF is actuated; printed p.45
! item 21 then suppresses the subject -- "Instruction Fetch (IF).  An
! attempt has been made to fetch an instruction from, or branch to, a
! location in the protected area of storage while the interruption
! system was enabled.  If the corresponding mask bit was one, the fetch
! or branch did not take place and the entire operation was
! suppressed."; and p.50 exception 2 permits the interruption, which
! lands at I6220.  I6220's "LX,$X1,$X1 / BZXVZ,SERS" then checks that
! the suppressed subject did NOT write $X1.  Falling straight through
! the EX instead reports "-NO INT TAKEN WHEN EX INST ABOVE UB."
!
! (Note that p.45 item 21 says "while the interruption system was
! ENABLED".  That is exactly why p.50's exception 1 has to exist, and
! why 1CB is a test of it: without the exception the whole rule is
! inert here.)
!
! EVERY HEADING AND ERROR MESSAGE, VERBATIM, WITH ITS LOCATION.  Read
! off the comment column at 500-600 dpi; punctuation transcribed, not
! tidied.
!
!   023442.40  -TEST 1C, CHK MISC CNTRLS OF EX.
!   023443.00  -TEST 1CA, ENABLE AFTER EX.
!   023445.40  -ILLEGAL OP CODE.
!   023447.40  -NO INT TAKEN ON EX
!   023450.00  -WHEN OP SET.
!   023450.40  -TEST 1CB, CHK ADR MONITOR.
!   023455.40  -NO INT TAKEN WHEN EX INST
!   023456.00  -ABOVE UB.
!   023457.00  -INSTRUCTION EXECUTED ABOVE UB WAS
!   023457.40  -NOT NOPED.
!   023460.00  -CLR BNDS REG.
!
! THREE error messages (each printed over two rows), five headings, one
! inline data word.
!
! NO DECK TYPO AND NO SCAN DAMAGE IN THIS RANGE -- and one reading has
! to be corrected to say so.  docs/execution_operations.md sec.3.1
! records the header comment as "-TEST IC," and says "the listing's
! comment column prints 1C as IC throughout".  Measured at 600 dpi it
! does not.  The glyph after "TEST " on the 023442.40 row and the glyph
! after "TEST " on the 023443.00 row are the SAME character:
!
!   row 023442.40   flag y381-387 x2766-2789 (LEFT of the stem only)
!                   stem x2781-2789
!                   foot y429-438 x2766-2801 (both sides)
!   row 023443.00   flag y475-486 x2768-2790
!                   stem x2781-2789
!                   foot y529-538 x2766-2803
!
! i.e. an asymmetric upper-LEFT flag plus a symmetric bottom serif --
! the printer's DIGIT 1, identical in both rows, and identical to the 1
! of "1CB" at 023450.40.  What looks like a letter I's top bar on the
! 023442.40 row is the DASHED RULE LINE that the listing prints across
! that row at y376-380 (it spans x2755-2814, wider than the glyph).
! The printer's letter I is a different glyph entirely: a BARE 13 px
! stroke with no serifs at all, as in the I of "MISC" on the same line
! (x3317, width 13) and as docs/listing_typography.md measured for the
! label column.  So the header really prints "-TEST 1C," with a digit
! -- and the comment column and the label column then AGREE about
! digit-versus-letter on the same page ("1C"/"1CA"/"1CB" against
! "I6218"/"I6219"/"I6220") instead of disagreeing.  THE METHOD
! GENERALISES, and docs/listing_typography.md does not cover it: any
! glyph verdict taken on a row this listing rules THROUGH is suspect
! until the rule's own x-span is measured against the glyph's.  Here
! the rule spans 59 px where the glyph spans 24.  (Whether the note's
! "ICB" at TEST 2CB on PDF p.103 is the same artefact was not checked;
! that page was not rendered for this file.)
!
! "CNTRLS" IS AN ABBREVIATION, NOT A DROPPED LETTER, by pitch.  The row
! is set on a fixed pitch of 60.6 px at 600 dpi (C of "CHK" x2999 to C
! of "MISC" x3423 is 7 cells, 60.6/cell; C of "MISC" to C of "CNTRLS"
! is 2 cells, 58/cell).  "CNTRLS" runs C x3539 ... S x3842 = 303 px =
! exactly 5 cells for 6 characters, so the six letters occupy six
! CONSECUTIVE cells with no blank between them.  A dropped O would have
! left a ~60 px hole and there is none.  The same holds for the deck's
! other abbreviations here (ADR, BNDS, CHK, INST, INDS), which are
! ordinary for this author.
!
! ONE MORE CORRECTION TO docs/execution_operations.md, and this one is
! the note's own transcription rather than a deck defect.  Sec.3.3
! prints 023454.00 as "TI,1,1000,$MASK".  It is "TI,1,I00O,$MASK".  The
! machine code settles it on its own -- the first printed group is
! "24503.00 80", a 24-bit BIT address in word 0o24503, which is the
! constant PDF p.114 labels I00O ("-INDX WD. OF ONES.") and which five
! other files in this directory already SYN at (8)24503.0; a literal
! 1000 would be a decimal bit address in word 15.  The glyphs agree,
! measured at 600 dpi on this very row: leading stroke 13 px (the bare
! letter I), then two 45 px digit zeros, then a 50 px letter O, against
! a 33 px digit 1 in the same row's ",1," count field.  That matches
! the calibration verify_ax4b_1246_transmit_swap_listing.py already
! established (letter O 49 px, digit 0 42-45 px).  The correction
! matters for more than spelling: an all-ones word into $MASK turns on
! EVERY settable mask bit, not "the relevant" one, which is what makes
! 1CB's asymmetry with 1CA legible (see above).
!
! ROW COUNT.  docs/execution_operations.md sec.3.6 says 1CA is "eleven
! rows".  It is TWELVE over 023442.40-023450.00 inclusive; the note's
! sec.3.2 table has eleven because the "I6218 BD,$+.32" header row is
! quoted separately in sec.3.1.  1CB is fifteen rows over sixteen
! half-words (the TI is a full word).  Twenty-seven rows in all.
!
! WHAT IS NEW HERE FOR THIS ASSEMBLER, checked by grep and knocked down
! before being claimed.  Only ONE first survives: "EX,$-.32" at
! 023447.00 is the repo's first SELF-RELATIVE EX, and its first
! BACKWARD one.  Grepping every verify_*.py and every programs/*.strap
! for an EX operand finds EX,I62B3-B6 and EX,I62EXT (label operands),
! EX,$X3, EX,$R and EX,$L (register operands) and nothing with "$+" or
! "$-".  Everything else was checked and is NOT a first: EX itself is
! witnessed by verify_ax5b_162_test1b_listing.py (five rows) and
! verify_ax5b_162_test1cc_listing.py (four); TI by nine files including
! the same "TI,1,<word>,$MASK" shape at verify_ax5b_16087_listing.py
! 021400.40 and verify_ax5b_160_test6_listing.py 022063.00; BZXVZ by
! ten files; SVA/LVI/LX/SX/SV/Z/SIC/BD/B throughout; the
! "DD(BU,32,8),(8)3740" word by ax5b_160k_constants.strap and
! ax5b_162_test3a.strap.  What the block ADDS, short of a first, is a
! MATCHED BIT-18 PAIR ON EX: 023447.00 has bit 18 = 1 and 023455.00 has
! bit 18 = 0, in twenty-eight half-words, so an implementation that
! dropped bit 18 for EX passes one and fails the other.  That is
! measured below, not argued.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: spelling only, none semantic.
!   1. The listing's "%...@" pair is this assembler's "(...)", and
!      "%8@DD%BU,32,8@,3740" is written "DD(BU,32,8), (8)3740".  The
!      "(8)" is load-bearing and not decoration: this assembler's
!      default radix is DECIMAL, so a bare 3740 assembles to
!      00000007234 (measured by ax5b_162_test3a.strap's header) where
!      the listing prints 00000003740.
!   2. Leading-glyph spellings follow docs/listing_typography.md's
!      measured result, so the labels are I6218, I6219, I6220, and the
!      externals I60SX1, I60S1, I62INT, I62ITE, I62EX, I62EXI, I00O.
!      SEN, SERS and BIT57 need nothing.
!
! EXTERNALS, SYN'd at the addresses other pages of this same listing
! print.  THESE ARE INPUTS TO THE CHECK, NOT WITNESSES -- the caveat
! verify_ax5b_16082_16083_listing.py makes about I6088.  A SYN row
! MAKES the address field come out right, so a row naming one proves
! only that every OTHER field lands where the listing puts it.  I6218
! is not evidence either: the SLC below is fed 023442.40 by this same
! transcription and I6218 is the first label after it, so checking it
! checks the SLC against its own input.  It is asserted as the block's
! anchor.  The genuine address evidence is I6219 and I6220, each the
! cumulative sizing of every half-word before it and each independently
! named by an LVI whose printed octal pair carries bit 18.
!
! THREE THINGS NEITHER THIS FILE NOR ITS CHECKER CAN SEE:
!   * I62EXI = 024332.00 comes from docs/NEXT.md's read of PDF p.110
!     and was NOT re-verified at any dpi here;
!     verify_ax5b_162_int_entry_listing.py stops at 024331.00, one
!     half-word short.  1CB's whole premise is that the subject lies
!     ABOVE the upper boundary 024322.00, and I62EXI is the only thing
!     establishing it -- but 023455.00's printed "24332.12 00" agrees
!     with the SYN by construction, so the row cannot catch a wrong
!     SYN.  It is at least self-consistent: p.109's label column puts
!     I62EXM at 024331.00, so 024332.00 is the next free word.
!   * WHAT THE 64-WORD INTERRUPT TABLE HOLDS WHEN 1CA RUNS IS NOT
!     READ.  1CA has no fill loop and neither does TEST 1B before it,
!     yet "SVA,$X1,I62ITE" with no "+.32" only works if the table
!     still holds I62K1 ("BD,I62ITE").  The last fill this repo has
!     actually read is 1CC's I6233 loop at 023522.00, which is AFTER
!     this block.  ONE CONJECTURE, FLAGGED AS ONE.
!   * WHAT WORD 3 HOLDS WHEN 1CA RUNS IS NOT READ EITHER.  1CA sets no
!     boundaries; 1CB's trailing "Z,3.0" is the first clear in the
!     range.  Irrelevant to the encoding oracle, but it matters to
!     whoever runs 1CA on the model: under exception 1 a stale
!     boundary would put the EX's own subject fetch under address
!     monitoring while disabled, and 1CA could interrupt for the wrong
!     reason and still look like a pass.

I60SX1 SYN, (8)23041.0            ! 1260's SIC return link, PDF p.41
I60S1  SYN, (8)22741.0            ! 1260's "CLR INDS 0-19" subroutine
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0             ! the SIC error-entry link
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0             ! the error-report routine
I62INT SYN, (8)24220.0            ! the 64-word interrupt table, PDF p.109
I62ITE SYN, (8)24320.0            ! "-ALL INTERRUPTS ENTER HERE.", p.109
I62EX  SYN, (8)24322.0            ! "LX,$X1,I00O" -- 1CB's upper bound
I62EXI SYN, (8)24332.0            ! the IMMED IX subject, PDF p.110
I00O    SYN, (8)24503.0            ! p.114 "-INDX WD. OF ONES."
BIT57   SYN, (8)24613.0            ! the boundary-control bit, p.114
        SLC, (8)23442.40
!
! ---- TEST 1C header row, then TEST 1CA ---------------------------
I6218  BD,  $+.32                 ! -TEST 1C, CHK MISC CNTRLS OF EX.
        SIC, I60SX1               ! -TEST 1CA, ENABLE AFTER EX.
        BD,  I60S1                !   clear indicators 0-19; returns DISABLED
        Z,   $IND
        LVI, $X1, I62INT
        SV,  $X1, $IA              !   point $IA at the 64-word table
        DD(BU,32,8), (8)3740       ! -ILLEGAL OP CODE.  actuates OP in passing
        LVI, $X1, I6219
        SVA, $X1, I62ITE          !   plant 1CB's entry in the landing word
        EX,  $-.32                 ! THE TEST: subject is the SVA above
        SIC, SEN                   ! -NO INT TAKEN ON EX
        BD,  SERS                  ! -WHEN OP SET.
!
! ---- TEST 1CB ----------------------------------------------------
I6219  LX,  $X1, BIT57            ! -TEST 1CB, CHK ADR MONITOR.
        SX,  $X1, 3.0              !   boundary control bit on
        LVI, $X1, I62EX
        SV,  $X1, $UB              !   upper boundary := 024322.00
        LVI, $X1, I6220
        SVA, $X1, I62ITE
        Z,   $X1                   !   so a subject that ran shows up
        TI,1,I00O,$MASK            !   FULL WORD: all settable mask bits on
        EX,  I62EXI               ! THE TEST: subject at 024332.00, above UB
        SIC, SEN                   ! -NO INT TAKEN WHEN EX INST
        B,   SERS                  ! -ABOVE UB.
I6220  LX,  $X1, $X1
        SIC, SEN                   ! -INSTRUCTION EXECUTED ABOVE UB WAS
        BZXVZ, SERS                ! -NOT NOPED.
        Z,   3.0                   ! -CLR BNDS REG.
        END, I6218
